===Verb===
{{en-verb|<,,took,taken>}}

# To [[assume]] [[control]] of something, such as a business or enterprise, and sometimes by force.
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=June 18 2013|author=w:Simon Romero|title=Protests Widen as Brazilians Chide Leaders|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/19/world/americas/brazilian-leaders-brace-for-more-protests.html|accessdate=21 June 2013
|passage=In Rio de Janeiro, thousands protested in a gritty area far from the city’s upscale seaside districts. In other cities, demonstrators blocked roads, barged into City Council meetings or interrupted sessions of local lawmakers, clapping loudly and sometimes '''taking over''' the microphone.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250901064728/https://www.nytimes.com/2013/06/19/world/americas/brazilian-leaders-brace-for-more-protests.html|archivedate=1 September 2025}}
# To [[adopt]] a [[further]] [[responsibility]] or [[duty]].
#: {{ux|en|He will '''take over''' the job permanently when the accountant retires.}}
# To [[relieve]] someone [[temporarily]].
#: {{ux|en|My husband is '''taking over''' the accounts department during the holiday period, while the chief accountant is away.}}
#: {{ux|en|If you will '''take over''' driving, I'd like to get some sleep.}}
# To [[buy out]] the [[ownership]] of a [[business]].
#: {{ux|en|Acme Motors is to '''take''' Jetcar Industries '''over''' this week, if all goes as planned.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-22|volume=407|issue=8841|page=70
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21579879-buy-out-firm-really-does-focus-operational-improvements-engineers Engineers of a different kind]
|passage=Private-equity nabobs bristle at being dubbed mere financiers. Piling debt onto companies’ balance-sheets is only a small part of what leveraged buy-outs are about, they insist. Improving the workings of the businesses they '''take over''' is just as core to their calling, if not more so. Much of their pleading is public-relations bluster.}}
# To [[appropriate]] something without [[permission]].
# To [[annex]] a [[territory]] by [[conquest]] or [[invasion]]; to [[conquer]].
#: {{ux|en|Ancient Rome '''took over''' lands throughout the known world.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|chapter=The Aegean Coast|editor=Karen Cure|title=[[w:Fodor's|Fodor’s]] Turkey{{nb...|The Complete Guide Including the Best of the Archaeological Sites and Beach Resorts}}|location=New York, N.Y.; Toronto, Ont.|publisher=Fodor’s Travel Publications|page=104|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/fodorsturkey00suga/page/104/mode/1up|isbn=0-679-02353-4|passage=Founded about 1000 {{smallcaps|bc}} by Aeolian Greeks, the city {{quote-gloss|Assos}} was successively ruled by Lydians, Persians, Pergamenes, Romans, and Byzantines, until Sultan Orhan Gazi (1288–1360) '''took''' it '''over''' for the Ottomans in 1330.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To become more successful than (someone or something else).
#: {{ux|en|Buzz Lightyear has '''taken over''' Woody as the most popular children's toy.}}
#: {{ux|en|Tiger Woods has '''taken over''' as the top golfer.}}
# {{&lit|en|take|over}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''took''' the car '''over''' to the garage.}}

